# SPICE MCP client - environment template.
#
#   copy .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is git-ignored; this file is not, so never put a real token here.
#
# The LLM is Claude reached through the **TAMU AI Gateway**. Authentication and billing are
# entirely the gateway's: one bearer token, no AWS account, no AWS credentials, no region,
# and no boto3. The token is read straight from the environment when the client is built,
# so it is never stored in the app's config object or written to a log or session file.

# --- required -------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Your TAMU AI Gateway token. Sent as an `Authorization: Bearer` header.
# This is the only credential the app needs.
TAMU_API_KEY=

# --- optional -------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The model id to ask the gateway for. This names the model *the gateway* routes to and
# says nothing about this machine: `us.anthropic.claude-opus-5` means the gateway reaches
# Claude via Bedrock on its side, using its own account - not yours.
SPICE_MCP_MODEL=us.anthropic.claude-opus-5

# The gateway root. Do NOT include a version segment: the SDK appends `/v1/messages`
# itself, so a trailing `/v1` here produces `/v1/v1/messages`. One is trimmed if present.
# SPICE_MCP_BASE_URL=https://gateway.api.tamu.ai

# How the model is asked to call tools. `native` (the default) sends the MCP tools in the
# Messages API `tools` parameter and reads back `tool_use` blocks; this is verified working
# on the gateway's `/v1/messages` endpoint. `prompted_json` puts the tool catalogue in the
# system prompt instead and has the model reply with one JSON object per turn.
#
# The fallback is not hypothetical, and it is worth understanding why: the same gateway's
# `/v1/chat/completions` endpoint, with this same model, accepts a `tools` array and then
# answers as though it had none. Tool support belongs to the *route*, not the model - which
# is why this is set explicitly here and never inferred from a model name (a typo raises
# rather than silently falling back).
#
# Check what a route supports with `python scripts\probe_tool_calling.py`
# (add `--prompted-json` to probe the fallback). Both modes call MCP through the same
# executor, so neither can write to a schematic without the UI's approval.
# SPICE_MCP_TOOL_MODE=native

# How much thinking the model does before answering: one of low, medium, high, xhigh, max.
# Sent as `output_config={"effort": ...}` on the Messages API.
#
# Unset by default, which omits the parameter entirely and leaves the model on its own
# default (`high` on Opus 5). Worth knowing what it buys: `usage.output_tokens` **includes
# adaptive thinking tokens** - on a typical one-fault diagnosis roughly 770 of ~1350 - so
# this is the only setting that reaches most of the output cost. Prompt wording reaches the
# visible answer and nothing else.
#
# It is not a substitute for asking a good question. Lower effort buys terser reasoning, not
# a better diagnosis, and a circuit whose fault needs arithmetic still needs the thinking to
# do it. Probe that the gateway forwards the parameter with
# `python scripts\probe_tool_calling.py --effort low`; if it does not, the app logs a warning
# and continues without it rather than failing the turn.
#
# `temperature` is deliberately not offered. It is not a parameter of `messages.create` in
# the installed SDK, Opus 5 rejects it with a 400, and it controls sampling variability
# rather than answer length - so it was never the lever for verbosity.
# SPICE_MCP_EFFORT=low

# Where per-session token logs are written. Relative paths are resolved from the
# repo root. The default is git-ignored.
# SPICE_MCP_SESSIONS_DIR=sessions

# Override LTspice's location if it is not at the per-user install path
# %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\ADI\LTspice\LTspice.exe.
# LTSPICE_EXE=

# DEBUG, INFO, WARNING. Server diagnostics go to stderr; stdout is the MCP wire.
# SPICE_MCP_LOG_LEVEL=INFO
